    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Alonzo Young opened a boat landing on the Yellowstone River near Park City in 1878, naming the post office Young’s Point.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mtmemory.org/nodes/view/128435"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Settlers from Ripon, Wisconsin arrived in 1882, planted elms and maples, and christened the area Park City.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mtmemory.org/nodes/view/128435"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Northern Pacific Railroad built a station named Rimrock near Park City, honoring the sandstone cliffs that rise north of town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mtmemory.org/nodes/view/128435"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Park City’s historic buildings are largely sandstone, echoing the towering cliffs that rise north of town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park_City%2C_Montana"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In a historic stand, 125 Nez Perce warriors held off cavalry near Park City, allowing the rest of the band to flee toward Canada.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visitmt.com/cities-towns/park-city"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Park City’s economy centers on farming, especially sugar beets and corn, which thrive in the fertile plains.",
